{
  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The training loader supplies one negative per position. With only a positive and one negative, HR@10 is always 1; meaningful rank diagnostics require a defined larger candidate set. Training diagnostics must remain distinct from the host’s fixed 99-negative validation evaluation.",
    "Context extraction is unspecified. Each target must be paired with its preceding history only; scoring an earlier-position target from the complete sequence could expose that target or later interactions.",
    "User-level bootstrap intervals are not implementable directly from batches whose user field is None. The report needs a reliable context-to-user mapping or must omit user-clustered uncertainty estimates.",
    "Recent deletion changes the final item, while oldest deletion preserves it. Their difference therefore combines recency dependence, readout-item changes, and position effects. Final-item-preserving shuffles help but cannot isolate all these effects.",
    "Candidate eligibility, tie handling, no-op shuffle counts, audit sample size, and runtime limits remain unspecified. Code difficulty 3/10 appears optimistic, and potential 8/10 is provisional without evidence that the diagnostics guide validation improvements."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Establish reproducible, nontrivial rank diagnostics without target leakage or confusion with benchmark HR@10.",
      "query": "In causal sequential recommendation diagnostics using shifted B×L positive targets and one sampled negative per position, how should target-specific history prefixes, larger fixed candidate sets, repeated-item eligibility, and deterministic tie handling be defined to prevent leakage and distinguish training-pair diagnostics from sampled validation HR@10?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Clarify what the interventions identify and add controls for final-item and positional changes.",
      "query": "For HSTU sequential recommenders, what controls distinguish recency reliance from final-item dependence and positional encoding effects when comparing recent-item deletion, oldest-item deletion, and final-item-preserving history shuffles? How should timestamps and relative positions be handled, and what interpretations remain valid under out-of-distribution perturbations?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Resolve dependence and sampling issues before reporting uncertainty or claiming inexpensive implementation.",
      "query": "For recommendation sensitivity audits with multiple overlapping training prefixes and repeated perturbations per user, how should user-clustered paired bootstrap confidence intervals and category macro averages be computed? What uncertainty reporting is defensible when the diagnostic loader does not expose user IDs, and how should audit sampling and inference cost be bounded?"
    }
  ]
}
